  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nSimilar today to what we experienced yesterday with high \npressure still present over the Upper Midwest. More diurnal \ncumulus as well as the atmosphere has heated, but lighter winds \naloft have preventing a repeat of yesterday's gusty winds. \nTonight will also be similar to last night with ample \nradiational cooling and some patchy fog in the cooler locations.\nThe high moving out and a shift in the pattern will make this \ncooler area mainly across eastern Minnesota and western \nWisconsin. For western Minnesota, there has been a shift in the\nrain chances for tonight with most of the rain now most likely \nto stay south/west of the Minnesota River Valley and be kept \nwithin southwest Minnesota and areas farther to the southwest. \nMaybe parts of Martin County could see rain, but overall we look \nto miss out on this round.\n\nMonday and Tuesday will be driven by the shortwave moving across\nthe Canadian Prairies with the fronts associated with it moving\nacross the Upper Midwest. There will be a warming trend Monday \nand Tuesday as warm air advection associated with the \ncirculation around this system occurs. This will come to an end \nas the cold frontal passage occurs, most likely in the later \nhalf of Tuesday. We could see some storms occur the later half \nof Monday into Tuesday morning associated more with the warm air\nadvection and warm front with the support of a jet streak. Any \nsevere chances here would be more in North Dakota and northwest \nMinnesota, but some chances extend further south into west \ncentral Minnesota as shown by the SPC marginal (1 of 5) risk. \nThe better chance for some more widespread rain and storm \nchances will be the cold frontal passage later in the day on \nTuesday. The main questions here will be familiar if you have \nbeen reading discussions over the past few days: timing of the \nfrontal passage and moisture. If we had more moisture present \nthis could easily be a fairly widespread event, but that still \nis a limiting factor in not just global ensemble solutions, but\nnow CAMs as well. On the timing side it will be locations with \nfrontal passage later in the day, as instability is able to \nbuild some, that could see convection come together. This \nshould keep activity generally across eastern MN and western WI.\nWith the questionable moisture rain will be very dependent on \nCI rather than the larger scale synoptic forcing of the front. \nSo this will be the usual some winners and many losers with \nrespect to the rain that we need. The rain deficit for the \nsummer continues to grow with drought now across much of the \nbroader region. Shear does not look great, so some doubt about \nhigher end severe and sustained deep convection. This lines up \nwith the SPC marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for areas along and \neast of I-35.\n\nCooler air moves in on Wednesday behind the cold front, with \ntemperatures still near normal. Rain chances related to some \nlift closer to the surface low will generally stay to our north \nacross northeast Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin on \nWednesday. Thursday into Friday morning higher pressure moves in\nwith quiet weather and near normal temps. Late Friday into the \nnext weekend is when our next system moves in. Differences \nbetween both the deterministic global models and their ensemble \nsystems still have NBM PoPs spread out over this period. This \nshould start to cluster more as it approaches to get a better \nidea of when this next system is most likely to occur.",
